Print the ggml device type in `--list-devices`.

ggml already classifies an integrated GPU as GGML_BACKEND_DEVICE_TYPE_IGPU,
and llama.cpp uses that at load time to ignore integrated GPUs whenever a
discrete one is present (see the "add integrated GPUs only if no discrete
GPUs were found" branch in src/llama.cpp). common_print_available_devices
drops the type, so a caller reading the printed list cannot apply the same
rule: on a laptop with a discrete card beside an APU it sees two devices and
adds a window onto system RAM to real VRAM.

Umbratic reads this list to size models before anything is downloaded, so it
needs the same distinction the runtime itself makes. Applied to the pinned
checkout during packaging, after the pin commit is verified.
diff --git a/common/arg.cpp b/common/arg.cpp
index 1f70d0a..2709a02 100644
--- a/common/arg.cpp
+++ b/common/arg.cpp
@@ -1157,7 +1157,13 @@ void common_print_available_devices() {
     for (auto * dev : devices) {
         size_t free, total;
         ggml_backend_dev_memory(dev, &free, &total);
-        printf("  %s: %s (%zu MiB, %zu MiB free)\n", ggml_backend_dev_name(dev), ggml_backend_dev_description(dev), total / MiB, free / MiB);
+        const char * kind = "discrete";
+        switch (ggml_backend_dev_type(dev)) {
+            case GGML_BACKEND_DEVICE_TYPE_IGPU:  kind = "integrated";  break;
+            case GGML_BACKEND_DEVICE_TYPE_ACCEL: kind = "accelerator"; break;
+            default: break;
+        }
+        printf("  %s: %s (%s, %zu MiB, %zu MiB free)\n", ggml_backend_dev_name(dev), ggml_backend_dev_description(dev), kind, total / MiB, free / MiB);
     }
 }
 
